      "text": "Given that the sum of two numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) is 6, and the difference of their squares is 12, we need to find the positive difference \\( x - y \\).\n\nFirst, let's express the given information mathematically:\n\\[ x + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\]\n\nWe know that \\( x^2 - y^2 \\) can be factored using the difference of squares formula:\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\]\n\nSubstituting the given sum into the factored form, we get:\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\]\n\\[ 12 = 6(x - y) \\]\n\nTo find \\( x - y \\), we solve the equation:\n\\[ 6(x - y) = 12 \\]\n\\[ x - y = \\frac{12}{6} \\]\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
