      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we let the two numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). According to the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe can use the identity for the difference of squares, which states that \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\). Substituting the first equation into this identity, we get:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) = 6(x - y)\n   \\]\n   Since we know that \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\), we can substitute this into the equation:\n   \\[\n   6(x - y) = 12\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x - y \\), we divide both sides by 6:\n   \\[\n   x - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n   \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x - y = 2\n   \\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations together:\n   \\[\n   (x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   2x = 8\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x \\), we divide both sides by 2:\n   \\[\n   x = 4\n   \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = 4 \\) back into the first equation to find \\( y \\):\n   \\[\n   4 + y = 6\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( y \\), we subtract 4 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   y = 2\n   \\]\n\nThe two numbers are 4 and 2. The positive difference of these two numbers is:\n   \\[\n   |4 - 2| = 2\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
